msgid "Write the narrative"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Tell the study as a story in chapters, written in the page itself.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The narrative is a node of the graph, saved in the ``.em.json`` with the "
"rest of the study. It is made of chapters, and each chapter holds blocks:"
" paragraphs that you write, and blocks that show a part of the graph (see"
" :doc:`embeds`). A chapter can narrate a *lane* of the graph, an epoch or"
" an activity; the lane is what the Matrix beside the page follows and "
"what an AI draft is asked about."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open a study. The Templu Mare file used in :doc:`/tutorial/first-study` "
"already carries a story, *The Great Temple: survey, analysis and "
"reconstruction*, with chapters such as *Virtual Activity 2: colonnade*."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click **Narrative** in the bar of the workspaces. The workspace shows the"
" **Index** of the story above the **Outliner** on the left, the "
"**Narrative** window (the page) in the middle, and a **Graph** window in "
"**Matrix** mode beside the **Inspector** on the right (see "
":doc:`/reference/workspaces`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Start a story"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When the graph has no narrative yet, the page says **This graph has no "
"narrative yet** and offers **Propose the chapters**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Propose the chapters**. EMStudio writes a first structure: an "
"**Introduction** with the name of the site, then one chapter for each "
"epoch, from the oldest to the most recent, each narrating its epoch and "
"showing its Matrix, with a paragraph marked «to be written». The standard"
" blocks **History of research**, **Heritage involved (HDT-O)** and "
"**Bibliography** are added at the start and at the end."
msgstr ""

msgid "The proposal is one step of the history: ``Edit ▸ Undo`` takes it back."
msgstr ""

msgid "Nothing else is written for you: every paragraph waits for your text."
msgstr ""

msgid "Write in the page"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Narrative** window has four readings in its header: **Write**, "
"**Read**, **Print** and **Notebook**. The Narrative workspace opens in "
"**Write**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click the title of the story at the top of the page and type; press "
":kbd:`Enter` or click elsewhere to keep it. Under the title, click **Add "
"a subtitle** to write one. The same two fields are in the **Inspector** "
"of the story, which a click on the head of the page opens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click a paragraph and write. An empty paragraph shows **(empty paragraph "
"— click to write)**, or the words «to be written» of the proposal, which "
"your text replaces. The paragraph is kept when you leave it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To add a paragraph, move the pointer between two blocks and press the "
"**+** (**Insert a block here**), then choose **Text**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To rename a chapter, click its title in the page, type, and press "
":kbd:`Enter`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each change is one step of the history, saved with the file by ``File ▸ "
"Save``."
msgstr ""

msgid "Add, order and remove chapters"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A new chapter is born where you write. In the **+** between two blocks, "
"**New chapter here** puts a chapter title at that point, and the blocks "
"that follow become its text. The new title is selected, ready to type."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Right-click the title of a chapter for **New chapter before this one**, "
"**New chapter after this one**, **Move up** and **Move down**. Right-"
"click the margin of a block for **New chapter from this block**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the **Index**, **Add…** offers **A chapter** (after the current "
"chapter, or at the end), the **Standard blocks** the story still lacks, "
"and under **Epochs not told yet** a chapter for each epoch that no "
"chapter narrates."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To move a chapter, drag its row in the **Index**, or drag the grip ⠿ "
"before its title in the page. A line shows where it will fall."
msgstr ""

msgid "To remove a chapter, select it and press **Delete** in its **Inspector**."
msgstr ""

msgid "Set what a chapter narrates"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click the title of a chapter. Its **Inspector** shows **Title**, "
"**Narrates the lane** and **Signed by**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In **Narrates the lane**, choose an epoch, or an activity listed under "
"its epoch, for example *II A.D. › VAct.03 Lintel of the columnade*. The "
"chapter shows the name of its lane beside the title when the two differ, "
"and the Matrix beside the page follows the chapter you are in."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **I sign** to sign the chapter with your identity. A signature "
"needs an identity: if none is declared, EMStudio opens the panel **Who "
"you are** (see :doc:`/how-to/rooms/sign-in`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**☆ make canonical** marks a chapter that a new proposal of the structure"
" leaves alone; the Index shows it with ★."
msgstr ""

msgid "Find your way in a long story"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Index** lists the chapters, with what each one still needs: the "
"paragraphs still to be written, and the AI paragraphs not verified yet. A"
" click on a row takes the page to that chapter. **Find in chapters and "
"text…** filters the rows and marks the paragraphs that match. **Story "
"coverage**, closed at first, counts how much of the graph the story "
"cites: the units of each epoch, the sources, and the reconstructions that"
" no chapter explains yet."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Narrative workspace with the Index of the story on the left, its "
"chapters numbered, and the page open on the first chapter."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read the story"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Read** shows the page as a reader with the link sees it, on white "
"paper. A click on a block that shows a node goes to that node."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Print** shows what the Word and LaTeX files will contain: the blocks as"
" figures or citations, and a gap, **does not go out: to be verified**, "
"where an AI paragraph waits for a person."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Notebook** shows the cells of the Jupyter notebook."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Print** and **Notebook** are previews computed from the story: you "
"write in **Write**."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A story that lives in the graph: saved in the study file, signed chapter "
"by chapter, with its lanes tied to the epochs and activities of the "
"Matrix. Two **Narrative** windows can show the same story in two "
"readings, one written and one read."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`embeds` puts the graph in the text; :doc:`ai-drafts` asks a model "
"for a first draft; :doc:`export-the-narrative` makes the files."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/reference/windows` describes the **Narrative** and **Index** "
"windows."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/explanation/narrative-as-graph` explains why the story is part of "
"the graph."
msgstr ""

